WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.317 --> 00:00:02.817
(sign swings)

00:00:02.879 --> 00:00:05.040
(cool music)

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.548
^- My name's Eddie Ulm, I went to Arizona State University

00:00:07.548 --> 00:00:09.100
^and I was part of Theta Chi fraternity.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.300
^- My name is Andrea Mares, I went to

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.190
^University of California Berkeley, Sigma Epsilon Omega.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.500
^- My name is Ryann, I went to school at

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.670
^Missouri State University, I'm a Theta Chi.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.410
^- Hi, my name is DJ, I went to Emerson College,

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:22.693
^Sigma Alpha Epsilon.

00:00:23.921 --> 00:00:26.338
(cool music)

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.470
- Fraternities actually have paddles to signify

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:33.040
the big brother little brother process.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.710
These paddles aren't necessarily tools,

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.043
they are displays or trophies.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.470
- It's like having a plaque, or like,

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.450
having something that kind of

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.750
lets you remember your time in the fraternity.

00:00:44.634 --> 00:00:47.051
(cool music)

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.413
- I think that a lot of fraternities do still haze, sadly.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.220
Most of it, I think, now, revolves around drinking.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.840
- In my experience, the hazing wasn't that bad.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.000
During my sophomore year, my organization SAE

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.130
abolished pledging.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:01.900
Like, yeah, we had to drink a little bit here and there

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.540
but it was mostly, you know, memorization,

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.540
learning, you know, the chapter history.

00:01:07.777 --> 00:01:10.360
(techno music)

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.840
- Fraternities are not business, they're non profits.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.310
So they don't make money in the sense of like,

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.210
we have this money and now we're keeping it

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.610
but I mean, fraternities have dues.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.220
- A portion of that due goes towards your chapter.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.550
Another portion goes towards your region

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:28.833
and your, some goes to like, the national organization.

00:01:30.117 --> 00:01:32.700
(techno music)

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.110
- They have the real estate, they have the time,

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:37.950
they have the community and when you combine

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.440
all three of those, you have a party.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.490
- My fraternity was actually a social one

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.910
and the main focus was to connect queer folks and allies

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:48.043
so a lot of it involved like, social gatherings.

00:01:49.143 --> 00:01:51.560
(rock music)

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:53.853
- Yes and no.

00:01:53.853 --> 00:01:57.200
You're gonna meet a lot, a lot, a lot of interesting people.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.430
Those people are your friends for life.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:01:59.750
- If you are truly being yourself

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.190
and you're around guys that like you for you,

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:03.320
you're not just paying for your friends

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:04.830
because they're gonna actually know you

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:05.780
and actually care about you

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.480
and know who you are and like you.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:08.630
Yes, you're paying to be in the fraternity,

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.740
but you're getting something more out of it.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.170
- Well, I don't think I was just paying for friends.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.030
I think that I was really giving my money

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.240
towards building a community

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.840
that I really felt at home with.

00:02:18.969 --> 00:02:21.552
(techno music)

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.920
- I do think fraternities get a bad rap.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.650
Some of them are deserved, some are not.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:29.480
Unfortunately, there are chapters that do terrible things

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.940
and don't care about being better people

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.590
and so those chapters get thrown in the news

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.910
and it makes it seem like everyone's bad.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:38.840
- As a whole I do think a lot of fraternities

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.270
a lot of chapters get a bad rap.

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.050
A lot of kids are really good kids

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.840
who put an emphasis on community service,

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.040
giving back to different charities and what not.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.050
There's a big reason why almost every chapter I can think of

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.120
has some sort of fundraising chair.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:56.280
- I don't think it should get the rap that maybe

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.550
people perceive it to get because it does

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.240
a lot of good for the individual

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.210
that is fresh out of, you know, their home life,

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:05.310
their home experience.

00:03:06.187 --> 00:03:08.854
(hip hop music)

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.500
- At my school, there wasn't a lot of diversity.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.620
That is just true in my fraternity.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.810
There was definitely a year when I was the only black guy

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.580
and for me, I didn't feel unwelcome.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.580
There were definitely awkward occurrences

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.020
and there would be moments when

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.020
people would say something that was offensive

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.910
or something that was like, borderline racist,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:24.820
and I would have to call it out.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.270
Like, there were definitely, it was a learning experience

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.380
for both of us because I had never really been in places

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:29.730
where I had to check someone

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.550
for saying something that was racist.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:33.710
It helped me grow as a person, it helped me like,

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:35.580
kind of understand my blackness more.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.580
- As a gender neutral transgender woman,

00:03:37.735 --> 00:03:40.470
I would say that my experience at my fraternity

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.020
was really unique.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:47.020
For folks who think that it's a super exclusive environment

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.300
and it's only built for a certain type of people,

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.467
I would say that's another assumption that people have,

00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:54.550
you know, and so like, really do your research

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.310
and make friends and, I mean, like, if you're interested

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:00.060
look for the people like you who are in those spaces

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.970
and of course, you're gonna have different views on things

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.530
but differences make us stronger.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.923
- As a minority, I felt very welcomed in my chapter.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.793
My school was not necessarily the most diverse school.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.020
While my chapter was definitely more diverse,

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:13.740
the majority of them necessarily weren't

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.401
so I think definitely seeing more minorities,

00:04:17.401 --> 00:04:20.650
having them not be afraid to join, of like,

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:22.853
you know, afraid of stereotypes and what not,

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.670
because you can actually have a really great time

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:27.320
and meet some really cool people.

00:04:28.478 --> 00:04:31.228
(positive music)

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:34.730
- It helped me manage different types of people

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.810
and manage being in spaces

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:38.710
that I'm not necessarily familiar with.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.610
- Definitely, I would say, the connections and the alumni,

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:43.110
alumni network that came with it.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.240
I came out to LA, I didn't know anyone

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.700
except for the friends that I met in my fraternity

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.150
in school, and I still am in contact and live next to

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:53.470
a bunch of my friends that were

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.200
my fraternity brothers in college.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.260
- I'd say Greek life was one of the most foundational

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.250
and fun experiences in my life.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:03.093
I value that time, second to none.

00:05:03.357 --> 00:05:06.107
(positive music)

00:05:14.643 --> 00:05:16.676
(sign swings)

